INT. KESTREL RIDGE RADIO OBSERVATORY - CONTROL ROOM - NIGHT

A circular room of pale concrete. Monitors in a horseshoe; a dozen cold, two lit. A coffee machine murmuring in the corner. The great dish outside is a black silhouette against the stars, motionless. Wind needles the shutters.

DR. EMERY ASHWORTH, 50s, cardigan, thick glasses on a lanyard, sits at the central console with one hand on a mouse and the other cradling a heavy black telephone to his ear. He listens with an expression of such perfect courtesy that it has stopped being an expression.

LYDIA THORNQUIST (V.O.)
(phone)
Emery, the board met this morning.

EMERY
Yes.

LYDIA THORNQUIST (V.O.)
They want the dish decommissioned by spring.

EMERY
I see.

LYDIA THORNQUIST (V.O.)
You see.

EMERY
I see, Lydia.

A long pause on the line. He turns his chair a few degrees. A green line crawls along one monitor and stops.

LYDIA THORNQUIST (V.O.)
Emery, you've been listening to that patch of sky for thirty-one years.

EMERY
Thirty-two in November.

LYDIA THORNQUIST (V.O.)
And in that time, how many signals?

EMERY
One.

LYDIA THORNQUIST (V.O.)
One.

EMERY
In 1998.

LYDIA THORNQUIST (V.O.)
And it was a satellite.

EMERY
It was a satellite that behaved strangely.

LYDIA THORNQUIST (V.O.)
Emery.

EMERY
Lydia.

He reaches over, with his free hand, and presses a button on the console. A second monitor wakes: a spectrogram, bright in the center, trailing off at the edges.

LYDIA THORNQUIST (V.O.)
What is that?

EMERY
Tonight's data.

LYDIA THORNQUIST (V.O.)
It looks like noise.

EMERY
It looks like noise at first.

He lifts a pencil. Taps the glass at three points in the display. Each tap lands on a bright line.

EMERY (CONT'D)
Three narrow peaks. Spaced exactly one hydrogen line apart.

LYDIA THORNQUIST (V.O.)
Meaning?

EMERY
Meaning nothing natural spaces itself that way.

Silence on the line. For the first time Emery lowers his voice.

EMERY (CONT'D)
Lydia, I would like to request that you tell the board the dish stays.

LYDIA THORNQUIST (V.O.)
How long has it been repeating?

EMERY
Eleven minutes.

LYDIA THORNQUIST (V.O.)
Have you told anyone?

EMERY
I'm telling you.

LYDIA THORNQUIST (V.O.)
Emery, if this is a joke...

EMERY
It's not a joke.

LYDIA THORNQUIST (V.O.)
If this is a glitch...

EMERY
It is not a glitch.

Wind needles the shutters. The monitor pulses, once, twice, in a rhythm so regular it hurts.

LYDIA THORNQUIST (V.O.)
I'm coming up.

EMERY
Lydia, the road is washed out.

LYDIA THORNQUIST (V.O.)
Then I'll walk.

EMERY
It's sixty miles.

LYDIA THORNQUIST (V.O.)
Then keep listening.

The line clicks. Emery sets the phone in its cradle, slowly, as if it might break. He turns to the monitor. Rolls his chair in until his glasses nearly touch the glass.

EMERY
(softly, to the screen)
Hello.

The pulse repeats. Exactly the same. Exactly as long.

EMERY (CONT'D)
Hello. I'm here.
